NETWORK DIAGNOSTICS AND REMOTE CONTROL

Run short, bounded read-only checks first.
  ip
  net
  net -a
  net -r
  cat /proc/net/wifi
  cat /proc/net/memory
  ping -c 2 127.0.0.1
  tcpdump -c 5

PORTS AND SERVICES
  mcpd      TCP 8080 Streamable HTTP MCP
  httpd     TCP 80 (user program, not kernel daemon)
  Telnet    TCP 23 (kernel service on Pico W)
  UDP control service   UDP 2000 (kernel service)
  scroll -u 2001        optional UDP LED text listener
Not all services are always started. Check ps and network mode.

LISTENER TESTING
  tcp_echod [PORT]
  udp_echod [PORT]
  nc -u -N 127.0.0.1 PORT
  nc -l PORT
Listener/echo server/long-running nc commands should be started
in an interactive terminal, not directly from MCP.

ADDRESSING
  127.0.0.1 is PicoNIX's loopback address. A host's own
  127.0.0.1 is NOT PicoNIX; it refers to the host.
  DHCP, STA vs AP mode, and link status affect remote access.
  Inspect /proc/net/wifi before changing network settings.

DEBUGGING CONNECTIONS
  1) check network mode, IP and RSSI;
  2) check process slots and server status;
  3) try a short get_temperature MCP call;
  4) avoid large output and indefinite commands;
  5) distinguish tool command failure from HTTP transport error.

SECURITY WARNING
  MCP has no authentication/TLS. Run mcpd only on an isolated,
  trusted LAN. Never expose TCP 8080 to the public internet.
